00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.596
Now, the construction phase is where we then take all these contacts
that we're building with AI agents from the inception phase

00:05:05.316 --> 00:05:10.996
and think about how can we apply technology to solve
these problems and actually deliver this solution.

00:05:11.676 --> 00:05:18.036
We first start creating a conceptual model based on the product
domain that we have defined from the inception phase.

00:05:18.436 --> 00:05:21.156
And then we ask ourselves, what are some of the cloud services?

00:05:21.156 --> 00:05:22.996
What are some of the design patterns?

00:05:22.996 --> 00:05:27.676
What are some of the architectural
components that we can use to facilitate this?

00:05:27.676 --> 00:05:32.756
And by then you will have a robust
framework or scaffold for the code generation.

00:05:33.916 --> 00:05:39.396
And from there, what we need
to do is to create the infrastructure as code.

00:05:39.956 --> 00:05:42.636
And then that goes into your deployment pipeline.

00:05:43.396 --> 00:05:46.876
Now you might be wondering if
you are utilizing so much AI and Agentic.

00:05:46.876 --> 00:05:51.796
AI. What's our role in this? As human engineers, as product managers,

00:05:52.796 --> 00:05:58.796
the collaboration model between human and AI is now
shifting towards more of a partner model.

00:05:58.796 --> 00:06:03.276
It's no longer having an AI system that's
super smart but knows nothing about your.

00:06:03.276 --> 00:06:09.516
Your entire enterprise context or your business,
but rather now treating AI as a partner, right?

00:06:09.556 --> 00:06:16.596
Can you give me some suggestions? Can you help me
break down this task so that we can build this together?

00:06:18.116 --> 00:06:24.316
Now, the key responsibility for human
in the room is to maintain the validation and decision making process.

00:06:24.756 --> 00:06:29.836
Because ultimately, we are the ones who are responsible
for everything that goes into production.

00:06:31.396 --> 00:06:33.396
And with that, I will pass the mic to Fabian,

00:06:33.396 --> 00:06:39.716
who will share more about how you can use Kiro
on our Agentic IDE to facilitate and implement this process.

00:06:49.436 --> 00:06:53.076
Thank you Derek. So now I'll be introducing to you Kiro,

00:06:53.356 --> 00:06:59.156
the AI integrated development environment
to bring you from prototype to production.

00:06:59.556 --> 00:07:06.956
Kiro accelerates every stage of the AI DLC
by bringing structure to your AI coding,

00:07:07.716 --> 00:07:14.156
and one of its key differentiators is its native
spec driven development capabilities.

00:07:14.636 --> 00:07:17.716
Now, I'm sure many of you have coded before.

00:07:18.436 --> 00:07:26.796
Yes. So coding, you just throw in a high level problem
and AI can quickly come up with a certain implementation.

00:07:27.076 --> 00:07:29.676
And that works great for prototypes.

00:07:30.156 --> 00:07:33.956
But what if you want to ship this next into production?

00:07:34.476 --> 00:07:39.716
That's a different story.
And that's where spec driven development can help.

00:07:40.156 --> 00:07:49.916
Spec driven dev is where you would focus first on the planning,
always creating the specifications before you go into implementation.

00:07:51.356 --> 00:07:59.356
So Kiro helps you do that by turning your prompts
into requirements system design as well as discrete tasks.

00:07:59.796 --> 00:08:01.916
So let's see a demo on how this is done.

00:08:02.716 --> 00:08:06.036
Suppose I want to create a new feature for an application.

00:08:06.556 --> 00:08:13.956
I will just clean the prompt and Kiro would then proceed
to generate the relevant specifications to just open it

00:08:13.956 --> 00:08:18.876
and you can see the specifications being created
starting with the requirements first.

00:08:19.516 --> 00:08:21.676
I can then look at the requirements.

00:08:21.716 --> 00:08:27.716
Each requirement having a user story
as well as a list of acceptance criteria.

00:08:28.396 --> 00:08:33.636
And I can go back and forth with Kiro to refine all these criteria.

00:08:33.796 --> 00:08:37.436
I can add, I can modify based on what my needs are.

00:08:38.436 --> 00:08:42.436
So over here you can see the relevant
acceptance criteria was being added.

00:08:42.436 --> 00:08:46.396
Once I'm happy with the requirements,
I'll proceed to the design stage,

00:08:46.796 --> 00:08:52.836
and this is where Kiro would come up with a comprehensive
design of the different component interfaces.

00:08:52.836 --> 00:08:58.276
And that includes things like API routing,
error handling, and even testing strategy.

00:08:59.436 --> 00:09:03.796
And finally, when I'm happy, I'll proceed to the implementation plan.

00:09:04.236 --> 00:09:12.076
So over here you can see a detailed list of tasks,
as well as a list of subtasks each mapped to relevant requirements.

00:09:12.716 --> 00:09:15.876
And here you can choose to execute all the tasks

00:09:15.996 --> 00:09:22.476
or just select the based on certain optional tasks
in order to have the quickest MVP build.

00:09:24.476 --> 00:09:28.916
So now let's take this one step further with AI DLC.

00:09:29.476 --> 00:09:35.676
So if AI DLC, there's this structured
human in the loop decision gates at every step.

00:09:35.876 --> 00:09:41.476
So Kiro will be actively asking
for your input for your decisions over here.

00:09:41.756 --> 00:09:50.316
Kiro is generating requirement decisions for a chatbot feature,
so it asks me questions on my chatbot capabilities, knowledge base,

00:09:50.316 --> 00:09:53.756
conversational features, functional non-functional requirements.

00:09:54.076 --> 00:09:57.636
And for each of these specifications,

00:09:57.636 --> 00:10:00.000
I can choose to accept its default recommendations
or accept an alternative, or even propose my own.

